uuid "3e2b0842-7869-5406-b40d-48cd2134b49c" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423623 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423626 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423630 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423638 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423640 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423649 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423649 1077 "selection" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423649 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423640 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423649 1079 #uuid "53026106-93b7-5112-b460-a4866b2a53df" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423650 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423632 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423650 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423633 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423650 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423635 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423650 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423643 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423650 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423646 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423650 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423650 1077 "selection" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423650 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423633 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423650 1079 #uuid "93222598-353b-55a9-b167-8027058d4fdc" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423651 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423624 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423651 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423627 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423651 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423628 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423651 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423636 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423651 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423644 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423651 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423651 1077 "selection" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423651 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423628 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423651 1079 #uuid "c2f630ec-9673-54b1-aa09-9f5f12f9cc84" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423649 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423652 1069 "q-74183" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423652 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal. Find $(u+2w)\\cdot (u-4v)-u\\cdot u.$" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423652 1072 #uuid "4593be43-7c77-5707-9a35-2724546ec310" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423652 1074 "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u+2w)\\cdot (u-4v)-u\\cdot u & =u\\cdot u+(2w)\\cdot u-u\\cdot (4v)-(2w)\\cdot (4v)-u\\cdot u \\\\  & =2(w\\cdot u)-4(u\\cdot v)-8(w\\cdot v) \\\\  & =0-4(u\\cdot v)-8(w\\cdot v) \\\\  & =-4(u\\cdot v)-8(w\\cdot v) \\\\  & =-4((-5)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & -8(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-3)+8\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-4(-5+9-8+0)-8(2-6-32+3) \\\\  & =16+264 \\\\  & =280.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423647 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423653 1069 "q-74086" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423653 1071 "Given the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $u?$\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423653 1072 #uuid "7ead0ce1-1494-5459-ab15-c85fe87f9dab" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423653 1074 "We compute the dot product between $u$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3+0-1+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3+0-1-2 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =6+0-2-4 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct option is $w$ and $x$ only." 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423648 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423654 1069 "q-39319" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423654 1071 "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ k \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423654 1072 #uuid "a297bc5c-e8a7-5968-a05c-32844e09a57a" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423654 1074 "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-3)+k\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-8)+(-6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =15-k-16-6 \\\\  & =-k-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k-7 & =0 \\\\ k & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423650 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423655 1069 "q-74158" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423655 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(u-2w)\\cdot (w-4v).$" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423655 1072 #uuid "c0dbf414-70a0-5f19-8395-cf58e2c89041" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423655 1074 "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u-2w)\\cdot (w-4v) & =u\\cdot w-2(w\\cdot w)-4(u\\cdot v)+8(w\\cdot v) \\\\  & =u\\cdot w-2(w\\cdot w)+0+8(w\\cdot v) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & -2(3\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & +8(3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(3-8-2+6)-2(9+4+1+4)+8(6+2+2+4) \\\\  & =-1-36+112 \\\\  & =75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423651 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423656 1069 "q-39293" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423656 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ a \\\\ 7 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423656 1072 #uuid "cc095c64-455d-525a-98ca-451d265b041d" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true] [17592186423656 1074 "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1+a\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 0+b\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ 2+4a-6b & =0 \\\\ 4a-6b & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-4)+a\\cdot 3+7\\cdot 5+b\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ -8+3a+35+4b & =0 \\\\ 3a+4b & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4a-6b=-2 \\\\ 3a+4b=-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-3,$ the second equation by $4,$ and adding the resulting equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n34b=-102\\,⟹\\,b=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=-3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a-6(-3) & =-2 \\\\ 4a+18 & =-2 \\\\ 4a & =-20 \\\\ a & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=-5+(-3)=-8.\n$$" 13194139534510 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0126d44d-cbd6-5e5d-8f9b-fd5306980a4d" 17592186423622 "answer-0ab9e3e6-5284-58be-a5fe-0054619d52b2" 17592186423623 "answer-1602525e-bf2f-5fd3-827e-a1e276978ea0" 17592186423624 "answer-1bfdcc5b-d056-5b31-b19a-05f23514f849" 17592186423625 "answer-2349f6f7-0b4a-5590-8339-7329d0015da4" 17592186423626 "answer-2a702037-a4b0-50cc-97fd-b5d6788b58a7" 17592186423627 "answer-314612f9-0892-5598-ae9d-b402b25a618e" 17592186423628 "answer-3546e267-373b-5e1e-9457-1de013551d43" 17592186423629 "answer-41f8727d-5688-5fe2-ac9f-115c4c0396ab" 17592186423630 "answer-586e0aed-67d2-521e-99a4-464bd6aafb33